From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eugene Feinberg <uge@persimmons.ai>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 09:44:59 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 05/37] LLHD Sig2Reg: narrow combinational drives and
 never-changing registers

Two more shapes that forced drives back to full width and made disjoint
writes look like collisions:

1. The narrowing was gated on the drive value being a seq.firreg, so a purely
   COMBINATIONAL drive never narrowed at all. OpenC910's ifu_icache_tag_wen is
   driven by concat(%new, extract(prb(self), 0)) with no register between.

2. A register whose `next` IS the register holds its reset value forever, so
   the narrowable shape lives in the RESET value, not in `next`. ct_prio*
   initialise an array per element that way:
     %r = seq.firreg %r ... reset %rst, hw.array_inject (llhd.prb %sig)[k], %c
   Reading `next` there just finds the register itself.

OpenC910 refused signals: 15 -> 8 -> 3. What remains is genuinely unpromotable
and was characterised before changing anything: two real latches (BUFGCE's
clk_en_af_latch, ct_mmu_dplru's write_num -- a latch cannot become a firreg)
and one real overlap (ct_mmu_ptw's lsu_data_flop, where one mux arm writes all
64 bits from a different signal while the sibling drive writes bits 59-63).

Modules holding LLHD after lowering: 3 of 503. ibex unaffected: core.mlir,
core_deagg.mlir and struct.mlir byte-identical, netlist unchanged.
---
 lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Sig2RegPass.cpp | 38 ++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Sig2RegPass.cpp b/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Sig2RegPass.cpp
index 2716f8e84..08d3be9a5 100644
--- a/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Sig2RegPass.cpp
+++ b/lib/Dialect/LLHD/Transforms/Sig2RegPass.cpp
@@ -409,10 +409,40 @@ public:
                 // register will hold, i.e. its result, not the
                 // combinational value feeding it).
                 if (offset.isStatic() && offset.min == 0) {
-                  if (auto firregOp =
-                          driveOp.getValue().getDefiningOp<seq::FirRegOp>()) {
-                    auto narrowed = tryNarrowFeeder(firregOp.getNext(), sigOp,
-                                                    firregOp.getResult());
+                  // The narrowable shape usually sits behind a register (the
+                  // drive value is a seq.firreg and its `next` carries the
+                  // pass-through), but not always: a purely combinational
+                  // drive can carry it directly, e.g. OpenC910's
+                  // `ifu_icache_tag_wen` is driven by
+                  // `concat(%new, extract(prb(self), 0))` with no register in
+                  // between. Gating on the firreg missed those entirely and
+                  // reported them at full width.
+                  auto firregOp = driveOp.getValue().getDefiningOp<seq::FirRegOp>();
+                  {
+                    // Which value carries the narrowable shape:
+                    //  * no register        -> the drive value itself
+                    //  * `next` is the reg  -> the register never changes, so
+                    //    it holds its RESET value forever and that is where
+                    //    the shape lives. OpenC910's `ct_prio*` initialise an
+                    //    array per element this way:
+                    //        %r = seq.firreg %r ... reset %rst,
+                    //             hw.array_inject (llhd.prb %sig)[k], %c
+                    //    Reading `next` there just finds the register itself,
+                    //    which narrows to nothing and forces full width.
+                    //  * otherwise          -> `next`
+                    Value feeder;
+                    Value selfFeedback;
+                    if (!firregOp) {
+                      feeder = driveOp.getValue();
+                    } else if (firregOp.getNext() == firregOp.getResult() &&
+                               firregOp.getResetValue()) {
+                      feeder = firregOp.getResetValue();
+                      selfFeedback = firregOp.getResult();
+                    } else {
+                      feeder = firregOp.getNext();
+                      selfFeedback = firregOp.getResult();
+                    }
+                    auto narrowed = tryNarrowFeeder(feeder, sigOp, selfFeedback);
                     if (narrowed) {
                       bool isArray = isa<hw::ArrayType>(driveOp.getValue().getType());
                       auto arrayTy = isArray ? cast<hw::ArrayType>(
